<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">J. R. Jayewardene</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1906–1996</span> · Also known as Junius Richard Jayewardene</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">J. R. Jayewardene (1906–1996) was a Sri Lankan statesman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-national-party">United National Party</a> who became prime minister in 1977 and, under the constitution of 1978, the country's first executive president, serving until 1989. He opened the economy to foreign investment and moved the parliament to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-jayawardenepura-kotte">Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte</a>. His rule saw the anti-Tamil riots of 1983, the start of the civil war, and the Indo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> Accord of 1987.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/j-r-jayewardene.jpg" alt="J. R. Jayewardene" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Department of Defense. American Forces Information Service. Defense Visual Information Center. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J._R._Jayawardene,_President_of_the_Democratic_Socialist_Republic_of_Sri_Lanka_on_June_20,_1984.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
